Martin Read wrote:
> "Boudewijn Waijers" <kroisos@REMOVETHISWORD.home.nl> wrote:

>> I'd advocate the opposite: abolish some scrolls of which there is a
>> spellbook or, preferably, abolish some spells of which there exists a
>> scroll.


> So, which of the spells that duplicate other items would you cut?
> (Cancellation is, I suppose, an obvious choice.)

Tackling the various categories:

> Spells for which a scroll exists:

I would abolish magic mapping. I am a map collector myself, and I think
a map is *inherently* made of paper, so they should only exist as
scrolls.

I would abolish identify. It is way too powerful. In my opinion, NetHack
is mostly about identifying stuff, and the spell and, less so,
price-IDing make that way too easy, the latter especially for wizards.

I would abolish taming. As a spell, it's too powerful. Having to find or
write scrolls would limit its power in a major way.

I would abolish create monster. The spell is currently used more as a
"create offer fodder". Alternatively, make summoned creatures no longer
carry items or be eligable for offering, or both.

> Wands for which a spellbook exists:

I would keep teleportation, but change the spell to teleport self and
the wand to teleport other.

I would abolish digging as a spell. The wands come about plentiful
enough.

I would abolish the spell of polymorph, since it makes it too easy to
create any object, even with the restrictions that are currently in
place for polymorph.

I wouldn't abolish many attack spells. They are the core repertoire of
the wizard, and should stay in the game, even if for that reason alone.
I count slow monster and sleep among these.

I don't really care about "cantrips" like light, secret door detection,
opening, locking. Their use is so limited, that I don't really mind
several ways of invoking.

I would abolish the spell of turn undead, and restrict it as a special
ability of priests. Good and neutral priests should get the traditional
turning, for chaotic priests it should either work as a charm undead or
as a revive corpse.

Contrary to what you thing, I wouldn't abolish cancellation. I don't
think it is too strong, and it doesn't provide many opportunities for
misuse, I think. I do see a good argument for abolishing it, though: it
would make it even more attractive to carry the wand, providing us with
even more "stupid mistakes"...  :-)

> Potions for which a spellbook exists:

Object or monster detection can stay: they are relatively limited in
their use. Restore ability is useless, both as a potion and as the
spell. A blessed unicorn horn is much cheaper in use, and almost as
reliable.

Haste self may stay: it is an attack spell, in my opinion (see above,
with slow monster).

Invisibility I have never used as a spell, so I don't know about that
one.

> Spells which do not exactly coincide with any potion, wand, or scroll:

As for healing, see below.

Fireball: see attack spells, above.

Cure blindness/sickness: unicorn horn (see above).

Protection: limited use. Once you can liberally cast spells, the few
extra points of AC don't matter anymore, and in the early game, you'd
probably need your spell points for more important spells.

Jumping: limited use.

Stone to flesh: this is potentially a very good spell, especially for
the early game. A good supply of food is guaranteed.

Create familiar: I doubt about this one. It is potentially a very strong
spell, but without polymorph, its use is limited. If polymorph were
somehow be restricted, it should stay. Polymorph traps should have a
limited number of uses.

Clairvoyance: limited use.


A new point to finish this mail.

A large part of the problem could be solved if a certain class could
only cast spells that are open to #enhance-ment. I would advocate a
change of the spell system comparable to #twoweapon-ing, where classes
that cannot advance in the skill cannot use it either. If this was
implemented, I would also propose to exclude healing spells for wizards.

